Chapter 10 — “The Land of the Lion King”
Chapter 10.36 — “The Great Fool”
From this chapter onward, the name “Licorice” has been changed to “Belalis.”
I had forgotten that there was a character with the same name in a derivative work, and because that could potentially lead to unintended overinterpretations about the characters being confused with one another, I decided to change it. Accordingly, I will also be correcting the name in previous chapters.
Sorry!
---
—Crusch Karsten’s heart was a crucible swirling with impatience and self-reproach.
She had no awareness that these impulses had been magnified through external interference. But even if she had been aware of it, the dark knot festering within Crusch would probably not have changed.
To say that something had been magnified did not mean that something which had not existed had been implanted within her. It simply meant that something which should have grown larger over time had been fattened by having that time skipped.
In other words, that “regret” was a sin that had been steadily growing within Crusch all along.
“――――”
Still carrying that untamable impulse within her, Crusch made a silent grind of her teeth.
There was a sensation of her teeth clicking together inside her mouth. And yet, no sound occurred—not outside her closed lips, nor even inside her own skull.
A world from which something that should have existed had been removed produced an intensely unsettling sense of wrongness. And her opponent was forcing the tip of his blade precisely into the gaps created by that discomfort.
“――――”
Flipping the knight’s sword in her hand, she knocked aside a slash approaching from the side, like the fangs of a beast.
Silent.
A stationary exchange of blows continued from there. One clash, two, ten—the steel of their swords collided, and countless sparks like blazing flames blossomed through the air alongside the impacts.
In silence.
Silence, silence, silence piled upon itself.
—Her breathing, the beat of her heart, the sound of her shoes stepping forward, the clash of swords, even the sound of her battle-hungry opponent licking his lips—nothing could be heard.
It was a sudden, abnormal battle that had assailed Crusch while she was moving alone through the castle.
“――――”
—That Crusch had been able to respond to the abnormality was entirely thanks to the blessing of the “Dragon’s Eye.”
While its power was released, these eyes could distinguish even the fine particles floating through the air. Thanks to them, Crusch had instinctively defended herself against the “Silence” ambush from behind.
She had realized it only after defending herself.
—If this enemy was the culprit who had abducted Ferris, then his current predicament was something that could have been prevented.
If Crusch had mastered the use of these “Dragon’s Eyes,” this situation could have been avoided.
“Then Ferris is still…”
Part of the frustration welling up inside her spilled from her lips, and Crusch’s eyes widened when she realized that it had actually become sound.
In that instant, her opponent caught up with the slight disruption in her footwork and swung his great blade—a single-edged weapon resembling one half of a pair of tailor’s shears. The blow grazed Crusch’s left shoulder as she failed to evade it completely.
“Gh――!”
“You’re slow, Crusch Karsten. You’d better keep your eyes properly on me!”
The small man with short blue hair leaned his face closer and spoke mockingly.
Shorter than Crusch, the man handled a pair of tailor’s shears nearly as tall as himself with ease, bouncing around the narrow corridor in every direction while holding his own against Crusch, who possessed the “Dragon’s Eye.”
Crusch opened her mouth, intending to sharply question her enemy about his objective, his intentions, and the countless questions that had sprung into her mind in an instant――
“――――”
Once again, Crusch’s voice failed to become sound.
The sounds surrounding her that had returned for only an instant were cut off again, and Crusch was returned to an incomplete world from which part of a finished painting had been scraped away.
It had happened again and again.
If there were simply no sound, she could rely on the vision of her “Dragon’s Eye” and manage things somewhat better. But when the vanished sounds returned irregularly, suddenly flooding her with auditory information like a torrential downpour from within the silence, it was impossible for her to continue fighting while completely ignoring it.
“Here—here there—there here—here――there!”
The sound came, did not come, came, did not come, came, did not come, came.
It repeated endlessly.
There were sword clashes that made sound, destruction that made no sound, failed attacks that made sound, effective blows that made no sound. Part of her consciousness was constantly stolen away by the need to sort through the information, and she could not put her full strength behind the tip of her sword.
As a result, the number of wounds inflicted upon her increased. She could not even approach an opportunity to counterattack.
For the first time since gaining the “Dragon’s Eye,” Crusch was being forced into a difficult battle—into a disadvantageous position.
“This――”
The weak voice that slipped from her in misery had its continuation erased by her opponent’s power, never becoming sound.
Was it supposed to be, “This shouldn’t be happening”?
Or was it, “This is no way to fight”?
Neither the enemy nor, more importantly, Crusch herself knew.
“You stubborn bitch! You’re gonna lose to me and end up yelping and crying anyway, so quit making me work for it and just hurry up and die! You’re noisy! Every damn thing is!”
The man swung his tailor’s shears in irritation, mercilessly slicing apart the surrounding floor and walls.
His voice had sound.
His rampage did not.
The dissonance produced by that fact unsettled Crusch’s heart. It was far too mismatched, far too unbearable.
—Including herself.
“Ferris――”
“――Nope.”
The question of where he had been taken was erased, and the soundless exchange of sword blows resumed.
Crusch parried, struck back, and knocked away the swinging shears, but she could judge the weapon’s condition through nothing but the sensation of impact.
Meanwhile, the man clicked his tongue in irritation.
“Tch――”
“You’re noisy, you’re noisy, you bastards are noisy, endlessly noisy, shut up you’re noisy, don’t breathe you’re noisy, don’t blink you’re noisy, don’t let your hair flutter you’re noisy, don’t stomp your feet you’re noisy, don’t swing your sword you’re noisy, don’t make your heart beat you’re noisy, don’t feel anything you’re noisy, your very life is noisy――”
“――――”
“Don’t laugh that stupid laugh――you’re noisy! Acting like some mother――you’re noisy! Don’t be born, don’t live, you’re noisy, noisy, noooooisy――――!!”
The man’s eyes were bloodshot as he rampaged furiously through the silence, drowning in resentment and rage completely unrelated to Crusch.
As she fended off that beast-like storm of sword blows with the aid of the “Dragon’s Eye,” Crusch was showered with flashes of malice for which she had no idea what she had done to deserve them.
She bit her navel in frustration.
Hostility, malice, even murderous intent were perfectly acceptable.
—So long as they were directed at her.
But they were not.
She was not even worthy of receiving them.
And so, she――
And Ferris――
“――――”
It was at that moment, when the flood of emotions and its explosion had nearly turned her mind completely white.
—A sudden blue light passed over Crusch and the man simultaneously as they engaged in a soundless blade lock.
“――――”
“Huh?”
The concern that it might have been the enemy’s next move disappeared when the man himself wore the same questioning expression.
The light had been unexpected to him as well. They had both been caught off guard.
And the voice that came from beneath the feet of the two who glared at each other from point-blank range moments later was the same.
What they heard was a distant, soul-filled scream, as though someone were vomiting blood――
“――Don’t die, you idiot!! What the hell are you thinking?! Don’t screw around!”
“――――”
The instant she heard that familiar voice, a sensation like every hair on her body standing on end swept over Crusch.
Relief arose first.
Then an immediate sense of duty.
Then anxiety and fear that had fallen behind them.
Crusch crushed all of those feelings beneath her and shoved the man in front of her away with all her strength. The man instinctively leapt backward, and Crusch’s sword flashed toward him――
The Hundred-Man-One-Sword was unleashed.
The inescapable sword wind swept through the corridor. The man clicked his tongue and swung his tailor’s shears, cutting through the ceiling and escaping upward.
He had avoided it.
――But an opening had been created.
“Ferris――!”
Crusch pointed the sword she had swung down toward the floor and furiously hammered it with a succession of blows.
The tip of her sword became wind, carving lines into the white stone like stars being drawn across its surface.
――Into that opening, the ceiling directly overhead was cut apart, and the man who had vanished from sight dropped a vertical cleaving strike down upon her.
Silently.
Crusch caught the blow with the sword raised above her head, and the man’s red eyes crossed with the “Dragon’s Eye.”
“Don’t get in my――”
The end of her voice was cut away—“way.”
But she did not care.
The result was the same.
The foot she raised stamped powerfully against the floor.
Immediately afterward, the fractured footing collapsed.
With Crusch and the man still locked blade-to-blade, they fell into the underground area that had been carved open beneath them.
Silently.
“――――”
—What awaited them below was a much larger underground space beneath the castle than Crusch had expected.
The traces left behind by the rampage of the “Demon of Melancholy” remained vividly even underground. In the vast space, large enough to host a ball or serve as a training ground, several figures stood scattered here and there.
Among them were Ferris, whose arms had been pinned to the wall, and Subaru, collapsed in a pool of blood.
“――――”
—In that instant, Crusch could think of no words other than red and black with which to express the emotions that had swollen within her.
The storm of emotions that erupted within her blew all rational thought and judgment into the distance.
――And the man took advantage of that opening, turning his body and landing a direct kick against her. She caught it with the knee she hastily raised, but the trajectory of her fall was forcibly altered, sending her flying toward the wall.
She planted her extended foot against the wall, sliding the sole along it to soften the impact before landing.
When she looked ahead, the man with the tailor’s shears stood on the opposite side of the circular room.
Between them, in the center of the chamber, stood a woman in a black dress—and for some reason, “White Ram” was holding hands with her.
The fallen Subaru was to the left.
Ferris, pinned to the wall, was to the right.
――The situation was far too difficult to comprehend.
Perhaps feeling the same confusion as Crusch, the man struck the floor hard with his tailor’s shears.
“Heyyy, what the hell is going on, Belalis?! Why’d ‘Detour’ come undone?!”
“Reborn! You came right away as soon as ‘Detour’ came undone… Could it be you want me that badly? Do you like me that much? Me too!”
“You’re impossible to talk to! Answer what I asked you!”
“If I stay quiet, I can’t answer, can I? Careless Reborn needs a dependable girl by his side. For example… yes! Someone like this Melty!”
“Mel――?”
Whether she intended to answer his question or not, the woman in the dress grabbed “White Ram” by the shoulder and pushed her forward toward the man.
Caught between the two who called each other Reborn and Belalis, “White Ram” widened her eyes slightly.
But her expression immediately became a cold smile.
And then――
“Nice to meet you, Reborn. I’m Melty, one of the ‘Beloved Children’ chosen by Mama.”
“What’s so ‘beloved’ about that? Belalis, you’re getting fooled so damn easi――”
“Mama, thank you as always. I love you more than anyone in the world.”
At the low voice woven from “White Ram’s” lips, Reborn caught his breath, and the anger vanished from his expression.
In its place came a powerful emotion of fear—and shame at himself for having felt it.
As he had heard, the relationship between the “Beloved Children” and their mother had been nurtured in a twisted manner.
Crusch could acknowledge that she had thoughts about the way that warped bond had been woven.
――But Crusch, whose thoughts had been painted over by emotions of red and black, had no room to spare them any consideration.
She kicked off the floor.
The enemies were directly ahead.
Reborn in the back.
Belalis in front.
And “White Ram”――
“Melty’s one of our friends! I think she’d be a great match for Reborn, so I’m rooting for them!”
With her back turned toward Crusch, Belalis wore a radiant smile as she addressed Reborn.
――That was fine.
It was genuine.
“White Ram” shook off the arm from her shoulder and spun around, facing Crusch.
She extended and contracted the short, cylindrical tool in her hand, gripping it as a single shooting staff.
Then “White Ram” filled her yellow eyes with a sharp determination――
“I’m one of the ‘Beloved Children.’ I’m your enemy, Miss Suirei!”
With that, she adopted a stance she had clearly practiced, pointing the tip of her shooting staff toward the approaching Crusch.
She stood in her way.
However――
“――The wind of lies is blowing, Melty.”
“――Yes!”
In an instant, “White Ram”—Melty—twisted her waist and spun around.
Matching her step, Crusch’s slash and Melty’s shooting-staff strike were unleashed together, crossing in a diagonal X.
They headed straight for the defenseless Belalis――
“Oh?”
The combined blow of sword wind and staff strike sent the woman in the dress flying head-on.
△▼△▼△▼△
—The “Wind-Seeing Divine Protection” distinguished lies from truth and saw through the falsehood in Melty’s words.
If her words were a lie, then her positioning itself must have been intentional in some way.
And in fact, following that intention, Melty had joined Crusch in landing a heavy blow on Belalis.
“――But I am surprised.”
In her usual behavior, there had been no lies. Rather, with her hesitant—or perhaps timid—manner, she had seemed afraid of interacting with other people.
――But that assessment had been mistaken.
Melty was brave, strong-hearted, and possessed remarkable courage.
Far more so than Crusch herself.
And with that――
“Now it is your turn.”
Crusch directed her sword intent toward Reborn, who stood beyond the fallen Belalis.
Reborn rested the tailor’s shears on his shoulder and clicked his tongue with a dangerous expression.
“You idiots… don’t come crying to me later.”
It was a somewhat nonsensical remark for someone angry about an ally being attacked.
But before Crusch could ascertain its true meaning, before the next battle could even begin――
“――Lady Crusch! Forget the enemy! Subaru-kun first!”
The cry jolted Crusch back to her senses.
She looked toward Ferris, who was pinned to the wall.
The object impaling both his arms above his head was one half of Reborn’s tailor’s shears.
Yet even while hanging painfully from his pierced arms, Ferris was not shouting about himself.
“Natsuki, Subaru-sama…”
That was right.
Subaru had collapsed.
His treatment――
No.
Shouldn’t the priority be eliminating the enemy?
Besides, what could she accomplish by rushing over herself?
What was needed was Ferris’s treatment.
Then obviously, Ferris’s release should come first.
Why couldn’t she make even such an obvious judgment?
She was foolish.
She was slow to notice things.
She was lacking in every possible way.
How could someone like this ever have been called the “Valkyrie”――
“――!”
An intense self-loathing, a pitch-black emotion, suddenly exploded within her chest.
She was about to drop her sword.
Her knees were about to tremble.
A sob was about to spill from her lips.
What was happening all of a sudden?
Why was it that, suddenly, her head, the depths of her chest, the most important part of her heart, all felt as though they were about to break――
“Love always comes with obstacles, doesn’t it? ――Being divided into enemies and allies is pretty exciting, too.”
She heard the voice mutter those words.
Lying on her back on the ground, the woman—Belalis—had clasped her hands over her chest.
Her voice was hoarse from the pain of her wounds, yet it was colored with passion for something beyond the pain itself.
And that voice awakened the black knot within Crusch in a form she could no longer suppress.
――I want to die.
If possible, I want to disappear completely.
An intense emotion utterly unbefitting Crusch Karsten began cursing herself.
“That’s why I told you――!”
“――!”
As Crusch was crushed beneath the impulse that made her knees want to buckle and her heart want to surrender, Reborn’s blade came crashing down upon her.
The sword she reflexively raised intercepted its path, but her weak, half-hearted stance lacked the strength to block it, and the impact sent her flying sideways.
Silently.
“This… ‘Collapse’ is getting stronger… Subaru-sa――”
Melty stepped between the enemy and the Crusch who had been knocked away to shield her.
The end of Melty’s words disappeared.
The strange silence inserted into the gap of her call was enough to bewilder Melty, who had never encountered it before.
And Reborn mercilessly exploited that confusion, driving his blade straight through the brave girl’s abdomen.
“――――”
Melty collapsed limply to the ground, and the shooting staff rolled from her hand.
Silently.
Blood burst forth.
Melty twisted her face in pain and screamed.
Silently.
Beside the fallen Melty, Reborn raised his tailor’s shears.
Silently.
“――――”
Without thinking, Crusch threw herself in front of the descending finishing blow.
Silently.
Following the trajectory revealed by the “Dragon’s Eye,” she swung her sword to protect her companion.
Silently.
The sword flashes danced in every direction as her exchange with the irritated Reborn continued.
Silently.
“――――”
Silence, silence, silence.
Focus, focus, focus.
Unwavering, unwavering, unwavering.
Self-mastery, self-mastery, self-mastery――
“――――”
There was a voice berating her, tormenting her.
A voice cursing her for making another mistake.
She had heard Ferris’s voice and ventured underground to save him.
But before Crusch arrived, Melty had already infiltrated the “Beloved Child’s” side and gained her trust.
Some sort of plan had been underway, and their own vision of success should have been taking shape.
Then Crusch’s unexpected intervention had forced Melty to change course.
The result was the girl lying on the ground now.
Subaru lying nearby.
Ferris pinned to the wall.
And herself, reduced to nothing but defense.
“――――”
If she had not come, would things have gone smoothly?
These past two months.
The MacMahon estate.
The Watergate City of Pristella.
Everything that Crusch Karsten had failed at.
“――――”
The black knot spread wider.
Even in the middle of battle, helplessness began to engulf her heart.
Eventually, once it covered her entire body, she would no longer be able to hold her sword.
Before that, perhaps the “Dragon’s Eye” would abandon her, and that blade would sever her lifeline first.
If, in the end, there was only a difference between sooner and later――
“――Raise your head! Crusch Karsten!!”
In that instant, a desperate voice broke into the silent world and set her arm, which had nearly stopped, moving again.
She parried.
Stubbornly.
She made herself parry.
Without any grace.
What had made her do it?
It was――
“We still have time! Nobody’s finished yet! And I’m not going to let anyone finish!”
The one roaring those words with his mouth wide open was Ferris, his yellow eyes blazing with anger.
For some reason, his voice reached her.
It shook her eardrums.
It made her sword move.
But the black knot flooding her entire body also gained strength from that voice.
“――――”
There was no one else.
He was the person she was least worthy of facing.
She had hurt him with words she never should have spoken.
She had left her sins unattended without any means of atonement.
She was on the verge of being crushed beneath guilt that could not be resolved.
And yet, Ferris raised his voice to that weak and foolish self of hers.
“Subaru-kun and ‘White Ram’-chan are fighting, too! Don’t give up! Keep fighting! Fight――please! Let me fight, too!”
Supported by the voice raining down upon her, Crusch somehow managed to resist the savage blades striking against her.
As she resisted, a voiceless cry caught in the back of her throat at Ferris’s plea.
He had said he wanted to fight.
――She understood exactly what he meant.
And her heart immediately screamed that it was impossible.
“It’s not impossible. It isn’t impossible. You can do it. ――Because it’s you, Lady Crusch!”
“――――”
Perhaps he had read her weakness from her expression or eyes.
His words were encouragement, as though scooping up her collapsing heart.
If it had been anyone else, perhaps those words would have been enough to stir their soul.
Yet the helplessness of being unable to meet the expectations placed upon her instead stripped even more strength from her.
The right to answer Ferris’s straightforward, devoted love belonged only to Crusch Karsten――
“I’ve known for a long time! Your memories haven’t come back, have they?!”
――In that instant, her breath stopped.
△▼△▼△▼△
I said it.
I finally said it.
Even though I knew that saying this would make her show the face I least wanted to see.
“――――”
Crusch stared at me in shock, her eyes wide.
Her irises were now divided into two colors: amber and gold.
I endured the urge to look away from her gaze and twisted my body.
Not to regain my freedom.
To put my voice into my abdomen and make sure it reached her.
“How long do you think I’ve been with you?! I knew! I saw through all of it from the very beginning!”
It was a lie.
――I had only realized because of these past two months.
At first, seeing her genuinely devastated by grief over Ferrix’s decision had been so painful that I could barely think straight.
Only after some time had passed and I had regained my composure, after the two months we had spent together acting within the “Six Tongues,” had I been able to form the suspicion.
“But I’m a coward, a cowardly and pathetic weakling, so I was too scared to confirm it.”
That, too, was surely one of the countless sins Ferrix had committed.
“――――”
While listening to Ferrix’s plea, Crusch knocked away the tailor’s shears.
There was no response from her.
Actually, for some reason, Ferrix could not hear a single sound from Crusch’s battle with the man wielding the shears.
Silence.
But he knew his voice was reaching her from the way Crusch’s expression grew cloudier and cloudier with every word.
He did not want to hurt her.
He had never wanted to corner her.
――But if this harsh treatment was necessary, then he would accept that sticking his finger into a wound was also a form of treatment.
Because otherwise, he could not save the life of this great fool who had bet everything on trusting a fool like him.
A great fool for whom there was no medicine and no magic that could save him.
“You keep saying you can’t, you can’t, and your head is all jumbled up, right? You think you can’t do it, and everything in front of you has gone dark, right? ――But let me correct one thing for you, Crusch Karsten.”
It was a phrase Crusch liked to use when speaking with others.
Deliberately using it now, Ferrix stared into those trembling amber-and-gold eyes――
“――You can do it. You and I can do it.”
Ah, what a foolish emotional argument.
What an unreasonable argument based on destiny.
No.
I hate this.
I really hate it.
I don’t want to look straight at it.
I don’t want to acknowledge it.
I can’t accept it.
This was exactly the same thing some other great fool had been doing.
――A great fool who arbitrarily placed his faith in someone, expected them to live up to it, and then threatened them with that trust as a shield.
It was no different.
But there was nothing to be done.
There was no other way.
That very same great fool had arbitrarily placed himself on the edge of a cliff where every second mattered――
So Ferrix had no choice but to use that great fool’s method, the method that had lit a fire in his own heart.
“――Lady Crusch!”
At Ferrix’s plea, at the look in his eyes, Crusch’s expression twisted violently.
Her lips moved.
She formed words that did not become sound.
She tried to say――I can’t.
“――You can!”
He could not hear her.
Therefore, she had not said it.
He forced that absurd argument through and held to it.
Her grief, her anger, her longing――he would deal with all of it later.
Before a life, everything else was trivial.
You can’t?
Why not?
――Because you are no longer the Crusch Karsten you once were?
“You’re not the person you used to be! I’m asking the Lady Crusch who’s here, right now!”
“――――”
“Let’s save it! You and me! This kingdom――the kingdom His Highness loved!!”
At his voice, the two-colored eyes looked directly at Ferrix.
A flame ignited within them.
The white blade of the sword was raised toward the heavens.
In the silent world, swirling wind became a blade and took shape.
It was the signature attack of the “Valkyrie,” Crusch Karsten――the saving slash that had once cut through the swarm of “Great Rabbits” that attacked her domain, changing their course and saving countless residents.
――With all her might, the Hundred-Man-One-Sword was unleashed beneath Kugelschreiber Castle.
△▼△▼△▼△
――Until the very last moment, the final moment of swinging the sword through, hesitation continued to cling to her.
“――――”
Her breath made no sound.
Her voice made no sound.
Even the sword flash and its results made no sound.
She merely witnessed the result of the blade she had unleashed.
That was the least she could do as someone who had forgotten Crusch Karsten while trying to impersonate the “Valkyrie.”
――The black knot that tormented and cursed her still writhed within her chest.
It had not changed the fact that it made Crusch’s hands and feet heavy.
The negative emotions would continue growing without limit, and surely by now they should have covered all of Crusch.
However――
“You fucking bastards! You and that other guy――neither of you is sane!”
Within the silence that denied the sound of footsteps, sword flashes, and every environmental noise, Reborn’s voice rang out.
As she cut away the tailor’s shears in his hands, Crusch realized that she had committed an act for which there was no possible response.
――Would the Crusch Karsten of old have done this?
She did not know.
And right now, that did not matter.
The Crusch Karsten who had been expected to do something by Ferris, who had been trusted by him, and who had answered that trust――was this Crusch Karsten.
And that self-recognition became a flood of positive emotion, filling Crusch’s mind and body with enough vitality to rival the black knot in her chest, allowing her to resist Reborn.
And while Crusch kept the enemy occupied――
“Seriously, Subaru-kun is such a huge idiot that maybe he should just die once.”
“Hey, hey. Don’t say something like that even as a joke. I don’t even want to think about it. Dying, I mean.”
Beyond Reborn, their blades crossed while the voices of two people traded insults.
Barely rescued from the brink of death, the boy and the “Blue” who had rescued his life were pressed together over the pool of blood, almost as though embracing one another.
Ferris held Subaru from behind while Subaru remained on one knee.
――It was obvious from the unwilling expressions on both their faces that this was not an embrace born of affection.
In fact, they had no choice.
――Ferris’s healing magic required him to make physical contact with the person he was treating in order to activate it.
“Actually, you’re the one who went way more overboard.”
“Same goes for you.”
With that, Ferris—the same Ferris who had allowed Crusch to cut off his arms and left everything below his elbows embedded in the wall—snorted as he looked at Subaru with a knife still protruding from his side.
――It was an exchange between two great fools.
Two fools who, quite reasonably, deserved to be told that neither of them was sane.
